1:# Standard Library (Stdlib)
2:
3:The `stdlib` module contains the **Miri Standard Library**, written in Miri itself.
4:
5:## Overview
6:
7:The Standard Library provides the built-in functionality available to all Miri programs. It sits on top of the generic `runtime` module, offering a rich, type-safe API for common programming tasks.
8:
9:## Architecture
10:
11:-   **`system.io`**: Utilities for reading and writing to standard input, standard output, and standard error (e.g., `print`, `println`).
12:-   **`system.fs`**: Filesystem operations via the `Fs` capability class (reading, writing, listing directories, etc.).
13:-   **`system.os`**: Environment variables and command-line arguments via `Env` and `Args` classes, plus platform information.
14:-   **`system.process`**: Process control (e.g., `exit(code)`).
15:-   **`system.string`**: The core `String` class, extending the runtime representation with methods for concatenation, comparison, and formatting.
16:-   **`system.result`**: The `Result<T, E>` enum returned by every fallible stdlib API.
17:-   **Collections (Planned)**: Modules for `List`, `Map`, `Set`, `Tuple` etc., implemented using runtime intrinsics.
18:-   **Math (Planned)**: Standard mathematical constants and functions (`sin`, `cos`, `abs`, etc.).
19:
20:## Design Principles
21:
22:1.  **Miri Native**: The standard library is overwhelmingly written in Miri, leveraging the compiler's own type system and memory management.
23:2.  **Idiomatic API**: Functions and classes should demonstrate the best practices of Miri programming (e.g., immutability by default, trait usage).
24:3.  **Automatic Discovery**: The compiler automatically knows how to resolve and parse `use system.*` imports directly from this bundled module.
25:
26:## The Implicit Prelude
27:
28:Three files under `system/` decide what every program gets without writing a `use`. Each lists only imports, so the compiler hardcodes no stdlib names.
29:
30:-   **`prelude.mi`**: re-exported by name and **reserved** — the rest of the stdlib is written against these types (`system.io`, `system.string`), so a program cannot declare its own type of the same name.
31:-   **`prelude_shadowable.mi`**: re-exported by name but **skipped** for any module whose types the program declares itself (`system.result`). A program that declares its own `Result` keeps it, and importing a module that needs the stdlib one is reported as a conflict at the import.
32:-   **`prelude_internal.mi`**: loaded for definitions only, so collection literals resolve their methods while naming `Array`/`List`/`Map`/`Set` still needs an explicit `use system.collections.*`.
33:
34:A module belongs in the shadowable tier only when nothing else in the stdlib depends on the types it declares.
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
// Copyright (c) Viacheslav Shynkarenko

use system.collections.array

runtime "gpu" fn miri_gpu_is_available() bool

runtime "gpu" fn miri_gpu_telemetry_reset()
runtime "gpu" fn miri_gpu_telemetry_uploads() int
runtime "gpu" fn miri_gpu_telemetry_launches() int
runtime "gpu" fn miri_gpu_telemetry_readbacks() int
runtime "gpu" fn miri_gpu_telemetry_fences() int
runtime "gpu" fn miri_gpu_telemetry_releases() int

/// Returns `true` when a GPU adapter is reachable on this machine, `false`
/// otherwise. The answer does not depend on whether anything has been launched
/// yet, so it is the same before and after the first kernel.
///
/// Asking creates no device: a program that checks for a GPU and then decides
/// not to use one pays for nothing.
public fn is_gpu_available() bool
    return miri_gpu_is_available()

/// Resets the residency cost counters to zero. Call before a pipeline to
/// measure its uploads / launches / readbacks / fences in isolation.
public fn gpu_reset_telemetry()
    miri_gpu_telemetry_reset()

/// Number of host-to-device uploads recorded since the last reset. A
/// `gpu`-resident binding pays one upload on the first launch that captures
/// it and none thereafter.
public fn gpu_uploads() int
    return miri_gpu_telemetry_uploads()

/// Number of kernel launches recorded since the last reset.
public fn gpu_launches() int
    return miri_gpu_telemetry_launches()

/// Number of device-to-host readbacks recorded since the last reset. Only a
/// cross-residency assignment (`let h = g`) reads back.
public fn gpu_readbacks() int
    return miri_gpu_telemetry_readbacks()

/// Number of host-side fences recorded since the last reset. Only a readback
/// fences; reuse and launch never do.
public fn gpu_fences() int
    return miri_gpu_telemetry_fences()

/// Number of resident device buffers freed since the last reset. A
/// `gpu`-resident binding releases its persistent buffer once, when the
/// binding leaves scope.
public fn gpu_releases() int
    return miri_gpu_telemetry_releases()

